          "id": "gen-u1-s3",
          "title": "Linkage and assortment",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Linkage and assortment</h3>\n<p>Genes on different chromosomes are typically independently assorted during meiosis; loci on the same chromosome may be correlated because they travel on the same physical DNA molecules. A testcross can expose gamete haplotypes without masking by dominance. For a double heterozygote AB/ab crossed with ab/ab, parental AB and ab classes dominate when recombination is less than one half. Two recombinant classes, Ab and aB, share the total recombinant fraction in a symmetrical model.</p>\n<p>For <strong>linkage and assortment</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>For 400 informative progeny and 64 recombinants, r=64/400=0.16, or approximately 16 centimorgans for a short map interval.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Score a blinded four-category testcross table, designate parental classes using phase information, and estimate recombination fraction with a confidence interval. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>linkage and assortment</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A recombination fraction of one half does not prove that loci are on different chromosomes; widely separated loci on one chromosome may appear unlinked.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>sex-linked inheritance</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Linkage and assortment: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>mendelian segregation</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>linkage and assortment</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>linkage and assortment</strong> using this design constraint: Score a blinded four-category testcross table, designate parental classes using phase information, and estimate recombination fraction with a confidence interval. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A recombination fraction of one half does not prove that loci are on different chromosomes; widely separated loci on one chromosome may appear unlinked.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>structural chromosome changes</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
