          "id": "gen-u1-s4",
          "title": "Sex-linked inheritance",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Sex-linked inheritance</h3>\n<p>X-linked variants have different transmission patterns depending on karyotype and which parent transmits the chromosome. In the common XY model, an XY parent passes an X chromosome to X-bearing offspring, not Y-bearing offspring; however sex development and chromosome complements are biologically diverse. An X-linked recessive allele can be expressed when only one functional copy is present. Carrier status, new variants and skewed X inactivation complicate textbook patterns.</p>\n<p>For <strong>sex-linked inheritance</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>For an X-linked carrier mother XAXa and father XAY, a chromosomally XY child has a one-half probability of receiving Xa under fair segregation.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Compare reciprocal crosses, assign explicit parental genotypes and chromosomal gametes, then score offspring by genotype rather than using appearance to infer sex chromosome content. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>sex-linked inheritance</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>Do not infer sex identity, phenotype, or an individual diagnosis directly from an abstract XX/XY classroom cross.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>mendelian segregation</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Sex-linked inheritance: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>probability in pedigrees</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>sex-linked inheritance</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>sex-linked inheritance</strong> using this design constraint: Compare reciprocal crosses, assign explicit parental genotypes and chromosomal gametes, then score offspring by genotype rather than using appearance to infer sex chromosome content. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: Do not infer sex identity, phenotype, or an individual diagnosis directly from an abstract XX/XY classroom cross.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>karyotypes and ploidy</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
