          "secNumber": "1.5",
          "heading": "Unit overview",
          "content": "Inheritance is a process of conditional transmission, not a lookup table assigning fixed outcomes to all parents. The same observed family can be compatible with several causal explanations. Start with a single locus and physically follow homologs into gametes before computing zygote probabilities. Next distinguish genotypes from phenotypes, and update family probabilities only after identifying the evidence on which a calculation is conditioned. The models here deliberately expose the assumptions hidden behind familiar ratios. No exercise uses an identifiable human family or a clinical diagnosis.",
          "simulations": []
