          "id": "gen-u2-s1",
          "title": "Karyotypes and ploidy",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Karyotypes and ploidy</h3>\n<p>Chromosomes are counted by centromeres rather than DNA mass. A replicated chromosome has two chromatids but remains one chromosome until sister chromatids separate. Ploidy describes homologous chromosome-set number, while C-value denotes DNA quantity. Human somatic reference cells are usually diploid, but cell-cycle stage, cell type and lineage can produce other configurations. Cytogenetic interpretation should therefore label cell-cycle stage and tissue rather than treating a microscopy field as an invariant genome.</p>\n<p>For <strong>karyotypes and ploidy</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>A human G2 reference cell has 46 chromosomes and approximately 4C DNA, not 92 chromosomes, before chromatid separation.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Construct a timeline of G1, G2, metaphase, anaphase and daughter G1. Record chromosome number and DNA mass separately, using metaphase spreads to calibrate the counting rule. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>karyotypes and ploidy</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A bright DNA signal cannot by itself identify the number of homologous chromosome sets, nor whether a chromosome is duplicated.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>aneuploidy and nondisjunction</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Karyotypes and ploidy: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>copy number and dosage</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>karyotypes and ploidy</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>karyotypes and ploidy</strong> using this design constraint: Construct a timeline of G1, G2, metaphase, anaphase and daughter G1. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A bright DNA signal cannot by itself identify the number of homologous chromosome sets, nor whether a chromosome is duplicated.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>three-point linkage order</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
