          "id": "gen-u2-s2",
          "title": "Aneuploidy and nondisjunction",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Aneuploidy and nondisjunction</h3>\n<p>Nondisjunction can occur at either meiotic division. Homologs fail to segregate in meiosis I, whereas sister chromatids fail to separate in meiosis II. These errors create gametes with missing or extra chromosomes whose effects depend on chromosome and organism. Viability filters observed outcomes; the proportion of abnormal live births is not the same as the formation rate of abnormal gametes. Mitotic nondisjunction after fertilization can generate mosaic cell lineages.</p>\n<p>For <strong>aneuploidy and nondisjunction</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>If a hypothetical error gives two normal gametes, one n+1 and one n\u22121, then the conditional abnormal fraction is 2/4, not the probability in a clinical population.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Use chromosome-colored model beads to simulate both meiotic divisions and record every gamete product, then compare those outcomes with independently modeled survival probabilities. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>aneuploidy and nondisjunction</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A model assigning equal outcomes at meiosis II cannot be interpreted as a population risk estimate without age, mechanism and ascertainment context.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>copy number and dosage</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Aneuploidy and nondisjunction: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>structural chromosome changes</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>aneuploidy and nondisjunction</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>aneuploidy and nondisjunction</strong> using this design constraint: Use chromosome-colored model beads to simulate both meiotic divisions and record every gamete product, then compare those outcomes with independently modeled survival probabilities. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A model assigning equal outcomes at meiosis II cannot be interpreted as a population risk estimate without age, mechanism and ascertainment context.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>marker association and ld</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
