          "id": "gen-u3-s3",
          "title": "Marker association and LD",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Marker association and LD</h3>\n<p>Linkage disequilibrium measures nonrandom association of alleles across loci in a population. It is not the same as physical linkage in an individual meiosis. Haplotype frequencies reflect demography, recombination, selection, ancestry structure and sampling. The commonly used D measure compares observed haplotype frequency with the product of marginal allele frequencies; r-squared standardizes squared association and is relevant to tagging performance. Genotype association alone does not specify the causal variant.</p>\n<p>For <strong>marker association and ld</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>If p(A)=0.4, p(B)=0.3, and p(AB)=0.18, then D=0.18\u22120.12=0.06.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Phase haplotypes in synthetic reference panels, compare stratified population groups, and examine how a change in allele frequencies alters D and r-squared even with the same underlying physical distance. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>marker association and ld</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A significant association marker may be a proxy for a nearby causal locus or for ancestry-related confounding.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>lod linkage evidence</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Marker association and LD: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>recombination fraction</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>marker association and ld</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>marker association and ld</strong> using this design constraint: Phase haplotypes in synthetic reference panels, compare stratified population groups, and examine how a change in allele frequencies alters D and r-squared even with the same underlying physical distance. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A significant association marker may be a proxy for a nearby causal locus or for ancestry-related confounding.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>functional perturbation</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
