          "id": "gen-u3-s4",
          "title": "LOD linkage evidence",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: LOD linkage evidence</h3>\n<p>A two-point LOD score compares the likelihood of pedigree genotypes under recombination fraction theta with that under theta=0.5. The logarithm is base ten, so LOD=3 represents a thousand-fold likelihood ratio for the specified evidence model, not a posterior probability of 99.9 percent. Genotyping errors, incomplete penetrance, pedigree misspecification and ascertainment affect the evidence. Multiple family scores can add if the families and modeled data are conditionally independent.</p>\n<p>For <strong>lod linkage evidence</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>For 20 nonrecombinants and 2 recombinants, theta_hat=2/22; the LOD curve can be compared to its reference at theta=0.5.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>For an intentionally simple phase-known meiosis model, calculate LOD(theta) from recombinant and nonrecombinant counts; compare the shape to a direct grid search and verify maxima numerically. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>lod linkage evidence</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A preferred theta is not a guaranteed disease-gene position; a full pedigree analysis needs family structure and penetrance likelihoods.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>recombination fraction</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>LOD linkage evidence: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>three-point linkage order</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>lod linkage evidence</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>lod linkage evidence</strong> using this design constraint: For an intentionally simple phase-known meiosis model, calculate LOD(theta) from recombinant and nonrecombinant counts; compare the shape to a direct grid search and verify maxima numerically. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A preferred theta is not a guaranteed disease-gene position; a full pedigree analysis needs family structure and penetrance likelihoods.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>mutation and sequence variation</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
