          "id": "gen-u6-s1",
          "title": "Allele frequencies",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Allele frequencies</h3>\n<p>For a diploid biallelic locus, allele frequency is counted from chromosomes: p=(2nAA+nAa)/(2N) and q=1\u2212p. A frequency is defined for a particular population, locus, time and sampling scheme. Heterozygosity, genotype frequencies and allele frequencies are related but not interchangeable. Frequency estimation can be distorted by genotyping error, nonrandom sampling and copy-number variation.</p>\n<p>For <strong>allele frequencies</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>From 35 AA, 50 Aa and 15 aa genotypes, p=(70+50)/200=0.60 and q=0.40.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Count genotype calls from a representative sample, apply call-rate quality controls and derive confidence bounds that reflect both binomial sampling and population structure. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>allele frequencies</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>Computing an allele frequency from a clinically ascertained set does not automatically estimate frequency in the general population.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>hardy\u2013weinberg model</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Allele frequencies: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>genetic drift</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>allele frequencies</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>allele frequencies</strong> using this design constraint: Count genotype calls from a representative sample, apply call-rate quality controls and derive confidence bounds that reflect both binomial sampling and population structure. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: Computing an allele frequency from a clinically ascertained set does not automatically estimate frequency in the general population.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>penetrance and expressivity</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
