          "id": "gen-u6-s2",
          "title": "Hardy\u2013Weinberg model",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Hardy\u2013Weinberg model</h3>\n<p>In an idealized randomly mating diploid population with suitable assumptions, one generation of random union of gametes gives genotype proportions p\u00b2, 2pq and q\u00b2. These form an equilibrium reference, not a claim that natural populations meet every assumption. Observed deviations can reflect inbreeding, selection, population structure, errors or nonrandom mating; no single departure uniquely identifies a cause. Allele frequency remains unchanged under random mating alone.</p>\n<p>For <strong>hardy\u2013weinberg model</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>For p=0.6 and q=0.4, expected AA=0.36, Aa=0.48 and aa=0.16.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Precompute expected genotype counts from a sample-specific p, examine whether expected counts are adequate for the selected test, and evaluate a stratified analysis before interpreting a pooled deviation. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>hardy\u2013weinberg model</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A failure to reject an equilibrium test does not prove absence of selection or error; low statistical power may mask deviations.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>genetic drift</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Hardy\u2013Weinberg model: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>migration and selection</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>hardy\u2013weinberg model</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>hardy\u2013weinberg model</strong> using this design constraint: Precompute expected genotype counts from a sample-specific p, examine whether expected counts are adequate for the selected test, and evaluate a stratified analysis before interpreting a pooled deviation. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A failure to reject an equilibrium test does not prove absence of selection or error; low statistical power may mask deviations.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>polygenic traits and prediction</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
