          "id": "gen-u8-s1",
          "title": "Crosses and experimental design",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Crosses and experimental design</h3>\n<p>A genetic cross is informative only when parental genotypes, scoring rules, replication and transmission assumptions are explicit. Reciprocal crosses can reveal parent-of-origin or sex-linked patterns; testcrosses expose gametes; controlled backcrosses isolate particular segregating regions. Blind phenotype classification and prespecified exclusion criteria reduce bias. Viability selection, maternal effects and developmental stage can alter observed categories.</p>\n<p>For <strong>crosses and experimental design</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>Under an Aa\u00d7aa cross with full penetrance and equal transmission, 200 offspring are expected to split 100 Aa and 100 aa.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Randomize growth trays, genotype parents with a separate assay, collect enough independent crosses, and report missing observations before combining offspring into pooled classes. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>crosses and experimental design</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A single large offspring count from one biological parent pair may not represent independent families or environments.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>pedigree likelihoods</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Crosses and experimental design: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>genomics and variant interpretation</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>crosses and experimental design</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>crosses and experimental design</strong> using this design constraint: Randomize growth trays, genotype parents with a separate assay, collect enough independent crosses, and report missing observations before combining offspring into pooled classes. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A single large offspring count from one biological parent pair may not represent independent families or environments.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>probability in pedigrees</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
          "secNumber": "8.1",
          "heading": "Crosses and experimental design",
          "simulations": []
