          "id": "gen-u8-s2",
          "title": "Pedigree likelihoods",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Pedigree likelihoods</h3>\n<p>Pedigree analysis compares the probability of observed genotypes and phenotypes under alternate inheritance models. Incomplete penetrance, de novo variants, mosaicism and measurement error require likelihoods rather than deterministic labels. Conditional probabilities depend on ascertainment: families recruited because of an affected child are not random samples of all families. Molecular verification narrows possibilities but does not remove uncertainty in interpretation.</p>\n<p>For <strong>pedigree likelihoods</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>With P(Aa)=0.1, sensitivity 0.95 and false-positive rate 0.02, the positive-test posterior is 0.095/(0.095+0.018)=0.841.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Code a tiny pedigree enumerator for a specified autosomal locus, document allele priors and penetrance, and compare the result to manual state enumeration. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>pedigree likelihoods</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A Bayesian posterior also depends on prior disease prevalence and test performance; a likelihood ratio alone is not an absolute risk.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>genomics and variant interpretation</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Pedigree likelihoods: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>ethics, privacy and inference</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>pedigree likelihoods</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>pedigree likelihoods</strong> using this design constraint: Code a tiny pedigree enumerator for a specified autosomal locus, document allele priors and penetrance, and compare the result to manual state enumeration. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A Bayesian posterior also depends on prior disease prevalence and test performance; a likelihood ratio alone is not an absolute risk.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>linkage and assortment</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
