          "id": "gen-u8-s3",
          "title": "Genomics and variant interpretation",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Genomics and variant interpretation</h3>\n<p>Sequence variants require quality, context, segregation and functional evidence to interpret. A read-depth decrease can represent a deletion, coverage bias or mixture of cell states. Population databases inform allele frequency but must match ancestry and quality criteria. Pathogenicity labels are evidence classifications, not arithmetic outputs of one annotation predictor. Variants of uncertain significance should not be treated as confirmed causal drivers.</p>\n<p>For <strong>genomics and variant interpretation</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>If 80 of 100 high-quality reads support a synthetic variant allele in a model tissue, observed variant allele fraction is 0.80, not necessarily germline copy number.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Independently verify read mapping, call filters, sample identity and genotype quality; compare functional tests and segregation information with explicit alternate explanations. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>genomics and variant interpretation</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>Variant interpretation in medical contexts requires qualified professionals and updated practice guidance; teaching models do not provide clinical recommendations.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>ethics, privacy and inference</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Genomics and variant interpretation: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>crosses and experimental design</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>genomics and variant interpretation</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>genomics and variant interpretation</strong> using this design constraint: Independently verify read mapping, call filters, sample identity and genotype quality; compare functional tests and segregation information with explicit alternate explanations. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: Variant interpretation in medical contexts requires qualified professionals and updated practice guidance; teaching models do not provide clinical recommendations.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>sex-linked inheritance</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
