          "id": "gen-u8-s4",
          "title": "Ethics, privacy and inference",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation: Ethics, privacy and inference</h3>\n<p>Genetic information is probabilistic, potentially familial and sensitive. Consent must distinguish research sequencing from return of results, secondary reuse and sharing with collaborators. An ancestry-informative association can reflect population structure without supporting biological race categories. Decisions about disclosure should consider uncertainty, re-identification, family implications and unequal access to interpretation.</p>\n<p>For <strong>ethics, privacy and inference</strong>, a model written without its inheritance assumptions is not a complete explanation.</p>\n<h3>A quantitative example with a defined denominator</h3>\n<p>A 1% population frequency cannot be used as the individual probability of a condition without knowing genotype, penetrance and ascertainment.</p>\n<p>Read the example in two passes. First solve the arithmetic as a hypothetical well-controlled classroom case. This separates a mathematical exercise from a causal scientific finding.</p>\n<h3>From the process to an experiment</h3>\n<p>Prepare a mock data governance plan defining consent scope, anonymization limits, data retention, access logging, and a procedure for withdrawing future use where technically feasible. Specify an independent biological unit of replication. Those are different controls and answer different questions.</p>\n<p>For <strong>ethics, privacy and inference</strong>, interpretation should follow the biology of the observation, not merely whether a significance threshold was crossed.</p>\n<h3>Interpretations worth separating</h3>\n<p>A classroom genetic model cannot determine an individual\u2019s identity, reproductive choices or medical diagnosis; avoid deterministic language.</p>\n<p>Compare the focal mechanism with <strong>crosses and experimental design</strong>, using the dedicated topic section rather than assuming these labels describe the same process. The comparison is not evidence that one causes the other. It is a deliberate stress test for the student's categories. State explicitly when none of these strategies is feasible.</p>\n<h3>Annotated investigation notebook</h3>\n<p>Start a new notebook entry titled <em>Ethics, privacy and inference: prior expectation, actual measurement, alternate mechanism</em>. Record the outcome before labeling it a confirmation or discrepancy.</p>\n<p>For the next pass, consider the topic <strong>pedigree likelihoods</strong>. Review its own mechanistic discussion before assuming a relationship. Ask whether it changes the apparent prediction for <strong>ethics, privacy and inference</strong>, changes only what is measured, or concerns a different scientific level entirely.</p>\n<h3>A second, more demanding application</h3>\n<p>The memo must discuss <strong>ethics, privacy and inference</strong> using this design constraint: Prepare a mock data governance plan defining consent scope, anonymization limits, data retention, access logging, and a procedure for withdrawing future use where technically feasible. What would a null result say about assay power?</p>\n<p>The main limitation for this subsection is not decorative fine print: A classroom genetic model cannot determine an individual\u2019s identity, reproductive choices or medical diagnosis; avoid deterministic language.</p>\n<h3>Connection to the next course topic</h3>\n<p>A later unit investigates <strong>mendelian segregation</strong>, whose underlying level of analysis should be inspected in that section.</p>\n",
